      "text": "To find out how far the tip of the second hand travels during a period of 30 minutes, we need to calculate the distance traveled by the tip of the second hand in that time.\n\nFirst, we need to determine the circumference of the circle traced by the tip of the second hand. The length of the second hand is 6 cm, which is the radius of the circle. The circumference of a circle is given by the formula:\n\nC = 2 * π * r\n\nwhere r is the radius of the circle. In this case, r = 6 cm. So, the circumference of the circle is:\n\nC = 2 * π * 6 = 12π cm\n\nThis means that the tip of the second hand travels 12π cm in one complete revolution around the circle.\n\nSince we want to find out how far the tip of the second hand travels during a period of 30 minutes, we need to determine how many complete revolutions the second hand makes in that time. \n\nThere are 60 minutes in an hour, so in 30 minutes, there are 30/60 = 1/2 of an hour. Since the second hand completes one revolution every 60 seconds, it will complete 1/2 of a revolution in 1/2 of an hour.\n\nTherefore, the distance traveled by the tip of the second hand during a period of 30 minutes is:\n\nDistance = 12π cm * (1/2) = 6π cm\n\nSo, the tip of the second hand travels 6π cm during a period of 30 minutes.",
